\
Let\'92s start with the commercial map. That will give us a foundation for a more detailed conversation about your first location.\
\
**The core business is subscription-funded family leisure, delivered through hospitality and supported by childcare.**\
\
That is how I would think about it commercially; the regulatory and tax treatment depends on the individual activities.\
\
The customer buys several things at once:\
\
- Somewhere their children enjoy going.\
- Somewhere they themselves enjoy being.\
- Access to trusted people who can give them a break.\
- A community of families at a similar life stage.\
- The convenience of combining several outings in one place.\
\
The distinction from your nursery is substantial. A nursery parent often needs dependable childcare to earn a living. A club parent must repeatedly decide that belonging is worth the money.\
\
**You are moving from selling necessary care towards selling a valued family routine.**\
\
Your childcare experience helps enormously with trust, safeguarding, recruitment and parent relationships. You would be adding expertise in hospitality, membership sales, programming and property management.\
\
OurHouse\'92s historical licensing materials actually presented a separate Ofsted-registered nursery alongside Jaego\'92s House. That illustrates how the two businesses can coexist, with different services and economics. [Hammersmith & Fulham licensing documents](https://democracy.lbhf.gov.uk/documents/b23757/Supplementary%2BAgenda%2BA%2B26th-Feb-2024%2B18.30%2BLicensing%2BSub-Committee.pdf?T=9)\
\
**The revenue engine has several parts, each with a different job.**\
\
| Revenue stream | Its commercial role | What can go wrong |\
|---|---|---|\
| Membership subscriptions | Provide recurring income towards the standing costs of the house | Discounts, cancellations and excessive included services erode contribution |\
| Caf\'e9 and dining | Generate additional spending and make visits enjoyable | A busy restaurant can still lose money after kitchen and service labour |\
| Paid childcare and activities | Monetise additional demand and specialist services | Staffing costs rise in steps; popular times become constrained |\
| Parties, camps and courses | Bring extra contribution and introduce prospective members | They can displace members from the spaces they pay to access |\
| Joining fees | Help fund acquisition and onboarding | Opening-period cash can disguise weak recurring economics |\
\
OurHouse\'92s published starting prices currently differ materially by location: \'a3105 per adult and \'a355 per child at Jaego\'92s; \'a3145 and \'a370 respectively at Jesse\'92s. For two adults and two children, that implies starting subscriptions of **\'a3320 and \'a3430 a month**, before additional spending. Those are useful reference points, not evidence that the same prices would work in your catchment. [Jaego\'92s](https://www.ourhouseclubs.com/clubs/jaegos-house), [Jesse\'92s](https://www.ourhouseclubs.com/clubs/jesses-house)\
\
Its current terms also reveal an important operating choice: Full House includes 20 childcare hours per child per month, while the entry House tier includes no complimentary childcare or class hours. Members or named caregivers must remain on the premises while their children attend. [OurHouse membership terms, clauses 11.4 and 13.1\'9613.2](https://assets.ctfassets.net/58xxl6j7f13w/4WqZxa9tH5pOXhXYA6UaKW/5b5f08daa844b2ecc27e825944383ab1/OurHouse_-_Full_House_House_-_New_Members_-_6_and_12_month_contracts_T_Cs.pdf)\
