**The most important financial measure is contribution per household.**\
\
First, distinguish three counts:\
\
- **Households:** the accounts that make buying and cancellation decisions.\
- **Individual members:** adults and children with access rights.\
- **Concurrent users:** people physically using facilities at a particular time.\
\
\'93Four hundred members\'94 is almost meaningless until we know which count it describes.\
\
At household level, the calculation is:\
\
> Subscription revenue after VAT + additional spending after VAT \uc0\u8722  costs attributable to serving that household = contribution towards standing costs.\
\
That contribution must cover management, the core staffing rota, premises, utilities, cleaning, systems, marketing and repairs.\
\
Here is a **teaching example for a compact leased club**. These are invented assumptions, not OurHouse figures, local quotations or a forecast for your business.\
\
| Monthly item | Illustrative amount |\
|---|---:|\
| 400 households paying an average \'a3240, including assumed 20% VAT | \'a380,000 net revenue |\
| Caf\'e9, activities, parties and other sales, excluding VAT | \'a320,000 |\
| **Total net revenue** | **\'a3100,000** |\
| Direct goods, consumables and variable delivery costs | (\'a310,000) |\
| Core payroll, including employer costs and market-rate management pay | (\'a345,000) |\
| Rent, business rates and service charge | (\'a315,000) |\
| Other standing operating costs | (\'a315,000) |\
| **Site operating EBITDA, after rent** | **\'a315,000** |\
\
EBITDA here means operating earnings before interest, tax, depreciation and amortisation. It is **not the cash available to take home**.\
