**For property, begin with the service brief and a capacity model.**\
\
Historical OurHouse licensing material described approximately 20,000 sq ft at Jaego\'92s and 30,000 sq ft at Jesse\'92s. These are substantial facilities. [Licensing documents](https://democracy.lbhf.gov.uk/documents/b23757/Supplementary%2BAgenda%2BA%2B26th-Feb-2024%2B18.30%2BLicensing%2BSub-Committee.pdf?T=9)\
\
For your budget, my initial search brief would explore roughly **6,000\'9610,000 sq ft for a compact format without a pool**, preferably with useful existing services and fit-out. That is a proposed search envelope, not an established minimum or confirmation that your budget will cover it.\
\
I would prioritise:\
\
- Secure childcare access and clear handover arrangements.\
- Acoustic separation between play, classes and adult work.\
- Comfortable caf\'e9 seating, daylight and ventilation.\
- Buggy storage, toilets, changing, storage and staff space.\
- Convenient access at actual school-run and weekend times.\
- A layout that works without expensive structural alterations.\
\
Kitchen extraction, drainage, power, accessibility, fire escape and lease obligations can change a seemingly affordable building\'92s economics.\
\
Planning also needs checking against the complete operation. Class E encompasses several relevant activities, but that does not settle every mixed-use club, building alteration or site condition. [Government planning guidance](https://www.gov.uk/guidance/when-is-permission-required)\
\
**The crucial property test is whether comfortable capacity exceeds financial breakeven.** If a building can serve only 300 households well, a model needing 334 to break even has a fundamental problem.\
\
**Croydon, Greenhithe and Essex are catchment hypotheses at this stage.**\
\
Your observation about parents seeking London-style amenities is a valuable starting hypothesis. We have not yet established local willingness to pay, competing provision or sufficient demand.\
\
I would assess small catchments rather than boroughs or counties. For Croydon, we would test particular neighbourhoods and practical travel routes. For Greenhithe, we would map the reachable surrounding catchment under real traffic conditions. Essex needs a shortlist of specific towns before a useful comparison.\
\
The question is how many households have the right child ages, disposable income, routines and willingness to travel.\
\
For illustration, acquiring 400 households at 5% penetration requires 8,000 **qualifying** households in reach. Five per cent is merely an assumption to test.\
\
We also need demand across the week. Maternity-leave parents, hybrid workers, caregivers and weekend families may want different services. A proposition that fills Saturdays but leaves the rest of the week quiet can struggle to support its staffing and property.\
\
**A lender will judge repayment capacity and recoverability.**\
\
I would expect a credit assessment to examine:\
\
- Proven cash generation after cash rent and a proper management cost.\
- Membership retention, payment collection and local demand evidence.\
- Downside performance if membership develops slowly or costs overrun.\
- Lease security, remaining term, rent reviews and repair obligations.\
- The sponsor\'92s equity, liquidity and operating experience.\
- Recoverable security: specialist leasehold fit-out may recover little on failure.\
\
A useful measure is **debt-service coverage**: cash available for debt service divided by scheduled interest and principal repayments. The lender\'92s required level depends on the loan and risk.\
\
Recurring subscription income helps, but annual fees collected upfront also create an obligation to provide future access. That money should not all be treated as freely distributable cash.\
\
Your nursery record strengthens the management case. It does not automatically prove demand for the club, and any proposed nursery guarantee or cross-security should be evaluated explicitly.\
\
**Breakeven and investment payback are separate milestones.**\
\
In our example, 400 households generate \'a3180,000 annual site EBITDA. Deduct illustrative annual central administration of \'a320,000 and replacement capital expenditure of \'a330,000, leaving \'a3130,000 before financing and tax.\
\
If total opening capital were \'a31.2 million, that implies:\
\
- A 15% stabilised site EBITDA yield on opening capital.\
- About 10.8% annual cash generation before financing and tax.\
- Roughly **9.2 years\'92 simple payback at stabilised performance**.\
\
That is scenario arithmetic, not an expected market return. Calendar payback would be longer with a ramp-up; equity returns also depend on financing, tax, later refurbishment and eventual exit proceeds.\
\
Likewise, the date of operating breakeven depends on opening memberships and net monthly additions. At 3% monthly household churn, a stable base of 400 needs around 12 replacement households every month before it grows.\
\
At group level, house-level profits must also fund central management, development and opening losses. A second house can increase revenue while reducing group cash generation during its ramp-up.\
\
Property ownership adds another return calculation. Levy reports that the Parsons Green building was let and subsequently sold to Little Houses Group for \'a321.5 million. That is a property transaction, not a club fit-out budget. An owner-occupied house should still be assessed with a market rent charge when judging its operating performance. [Levy transaction account](https://www.levyrealestate.co.uk/case-studies/retail-little-houses-group/)\
\
**For you, the first decision is which weekly problem the club will solve well enough to earn a recurring fee.**\
\
My working proposition would be a neighbourhood family club offering excellent play, good coffee and simple food, a flexible activity studio, quiet adult space and dependable bookable childcare. I would test paid repeat use and willingness to subscribe while your nursery continues operating.\
\
Your capital budget must cover fit-out, deposits, professional fees, pre-opening payroll, contingency and losses during the membership ramp. At the lower end, an existing fitted venue or paid pilot becomes particularly valuable.\
\
The next discussion should focus on **the customer\'92s week and the membership offer**: who attends, when, what they can reliably book, and what they will pay. Those choices will determine the staffing, building and funding you actually need.\
